- 드리프트 튜닝 관찰 (유저 육안, V6 세션 중): 입자가 한 방향으로 흘러 한곳에 모임 — 원인: SP_DriftBob 소프트 박스(±800/±300 하드코딩) < 스폰 박스(±1500/±600) 불일치 + 커얼노이즈 저주파(0.004)의 준균일 필드. 수정안: 스폰 박스 1600×1600×600 정합 + 주파수 0.012. **① 픽스 후 재조립 시 반영할 것.**

## 12. GPU User-array DI binding SOLVED (rounds 5-7, gate #5 PASS)

Problem: User array DI parameters (`User.CharPositions` etc.) linked directly into scratch-pad custom HLSL compiled clean but bound a node-local empty DI at runtime — GPU emitters never saw injected data (kill-probe gates #1-#4 all failed, num_particles unchanged).

Root cause (engine source, NiagaraHlslTranslator.cpp:7886 / NiagaraResolveDIHelpers.cpp:51-59): runtime DI resolution is name-driven via `RegisteredParameterMapRead == "User.X"` in the compiled DataInterfaceInfo. That string is only produced when the value reaches the function as a **ParameterMapGet read of `User.X` wired into the stack function call's override ParameterMapSet node** (the editor "Link Inputs" pattern). A direct UNiagaraNodeInput link compiles but registers no parameter-map read, so the resolver binds an empty default DI.

Fix (UnrealMCP plugin C++, uncommitted rounds 5-7 now in this commit):
- `UnrealMCPNiagaraCommands_Part4.cpp` — `insert_niagara_scratch_pad_custom_hlsl_for_pin` user_parameter_inputs linked path now authors SP-internal `NiagaraNodeParameterMapGet` nodes reading `Module.<InputName>` (stock-module idiom), output pin created via exported `CreatePin` + `UEdGraphSchema_Niagara::TypeDefinitionToPinType` (AddParameter/RequestNewTypedPin are unexported).
- `UnrealMCPNiagaraCommands_Part3.cpp` — stack-insert handler auto-bind: walks the SP graph for `Module.*` DI MapGet pins, and for each matching `User.<name>` exposed parameter builds the aliased handle (`CreateAliasedModuleParameterHandle`) and calls `GetOrCreateStackFunctionInputOverridePin` + `SetLinkedParameterValueForFunctionInput` — i.e. the exact editor Link-Inputs override structure. Reports `auto_bound_user_data_interface_input_count`.

Verification — kill-probe gate #5 (2026-07-11): SP_KillProbe (`CharPositions.Length(N); if (N>0) Lifetime=0`) at stack index 6, auto_bound=1, compile clean. Fresh actor, baseline ~209 particles; injected `User.CharPositions=[(0,0,400)]` via NiagaraDataInterfaceArrayFunctionLibrary → num_particles collapsed 209 → 1 (total_spawned 208 unchanged). GPU-side array visibility confirmed. Kill probe module + scratch script removed afterwards; V8 saved clean with SP_CharInteract_0 (auto_bound=3) at index 5.

Remaining for M2 verdict: user PIE retest of BP_WaterBlobManager (attraction/scatter), then restart gate.

## 13. M2 character interaction PASS (2026-07-11)

Scatter initially failed in PIE while attraction worked. Diagnosis chain:
- Structure audit: BP Set-array calls, MakeArray wiring, and all three stack DI overrides (`Module.CharPositions/CharVelocities/CharCapsules` → `User.*` ParameterMapGet links) verified correct.
- Velocity kill probe (same technique as gate #5, reading `User.CharVelocities`): temp `SP_VelProbe` killed 208 → 1 particles on injecting `[(600,0,0)]` — GPU velocity-array binding confirmed working. Probe module + script removed afterwards.
- Remaining suspect confirmed: the PIE pawn's `GetVelocity` effectively returns 0, so the shader always saw a stationary character.

Fixes:
- `SP_CharInteract` HLSL retuned: ScatterStrength 300 → 2000, ScatterVelThreshold 350 → 250, scatter zone widened to full AttractRadius (400), scatter branch now suppresses attraction (else-if) and pushes with an upward bias (`normalize(Dir + (0,0,0.5))`).
- `BP_WaterBlobManager`: replaced `GetVelocity` with pawn-agnostic position-delta velocity — new `PrevPos` vector variable; per tick `(GetActorLocation − PrevPos) / DeltaSeconds` feeds `User.CharVelocities`, then `PrevPos` updates at chain end. Compiled clean, saved.
- Known cosmetic nit: first tick after spawn sees a large velocity spike (PrevPos starts at origin); clamp later during tuning.

User PIE verdict: attraction near the idle character AND scatter when running through both work ("좋다 잘튕겨나가").

Tooling note: the MCP client coerces the literal pin name `[0]` to a JSON list, so `connect_blueprint_graph_pins` cannot address MakeArray element pins directly; route such calls through `execute_batch` (raw bridge params) as done here.

