`BP_WaterBlobManager` now has 5 instance-editable floats under "WaterBlob Tuning" — `AttractRadius` (400), `AttractStrength` (120), `ScatterVelThreshold` (250), `ScatterStrength` (1200), `SeparationSpread` (60) — each injected every tick via `SetNiagaraVariableFloat("User.<name>", var)` chained after the PrevPos update. The actor's detail panel is now the single tuning surface; BP values override asset User-param defaults at runtime. Compile clean, saved; fresh spawn 208 live particles with all 5 defaults verified on the instance.

## 18. M2.5 spline spawn zone (2026-07-11)

Replaces the temporary 16m box spawn with a user-drawn closed-spline polygon zone; box remains the fallback.

- **`User.SpawnPolygon`** (Position Array) + **`SP_PolygonLocation`** appended to the Particle Spawn stack after BoxLocation. HLSL: if polygon has ≥3 points, up to 16 rejection-sampling attempts inside the polygon's 2D bounding box with an even-odd point-in-polygon edge test (per-particle deterministic hashes, per-attempt salt); fallback to bbox-center on exhaustion; Z = polygon average Z + lerp(SpawnHeightMin, SpawnHeightMax, hash). If <3 points, Particles.Position passes through untouched (BoxLocation result — editor preview and unconfigured instances keep working).
- **`User.SpawnHeightMin` (100) / `User.SpawnHeightMax` (500)** — module-input links (same value-float pattern as section 14).
- **BP**: `SpawnZone` SplineComponent (bClosedLoop=true) on BP_WaterBlobManager. BeginPlay: clear `SpawnPolyPoints` → ForLoop over spline points → `GetLocationAtSplinePoint(World)` → array add → `SetNiagaraArrayPosition("User.SpawnPolygon")`. Heights exposed as instance-editable tuning floats injected per tick. Level instance seeded with a 14m×10m quad at Z=0.
- **Gates**: Niagara + BP compile clean; polygon-injection smoke on a fresh actor re-burst 208 particles cleanly (system reinit on array set); spawn rate verified ≈2/s.
- **Measurement note (editor-only artifact)**: with the editor backgrounded, freeze/resume ticking delivers large delta times, so SpawnRate dumps big interpolated batches and editor-side re-activations re-fire the burst; with effectively infinite particle lifetime these accumulate (observed 420→950 counts on a churned instance). Not a content bug — PIE activates once and ticks continuously. Gate measurements must run inside a continuous realtime window on a fresh actor. Population self-regulation (보강 2) will make this moot later.

## 19. Drift containment now follows the spawn polygon (2026-07-11)

User visual finding: spawning matched the spline shape, but particles immediately collapsed into a rectangle inside it. Cause: `SP_DriftBob`'s legacy hardcoded soft containment box (±800, ±800, ±300 around the origin) — any spline zone larger than 16m×16m pushed particles back into that box.

Fix: `SP_DriftBob` rebuilt with `SpawnPolygon` (auto-bound DI) + `SpawnHeightMin/Max` (User-linked floats). When the polygon has ≥3 points, containment is polygon-based: per-particle even-odd point-in-polygon test on XY; outside → fixed 120 accel toward the polygon centroid; Z held to the [avgZ+HeightMin, avgZ+HeightMax] band with the original proportional springs. With <3 points the legacy box branch is preserved (editor/unconfigured fallback). Bob/updraft logic unchanged; module reinserted at update stack index 2.

Note: centroid-seeking is approximate for strongly concave zones (a particle outside a notch is pulled straight toward the centroid and may cut the notch corner) — acceptable for soft containment; revisit only if a real level zone shows artifacts.

Gates: compile clean; live check on the user's 6-point concave zone — 202 particles active post-reactivation with the polygon injected.

## 20. M3-v1 burst trigger: pivot, tooling saga, and gate (2026-07-11)

### Pivot: hit droplets become the fragments

M3 originally planned a separate `Fragments` emitter (5-way split via SpawnParticlesFromOtherEmitter). Two hard blockers killed that route for v1:

- **Translator token-check limitation (fundamental)**: custom-HLSL node outputs feeding MapSet pins for *new* `System.*` / `Emitter.*` namespaced variables fail `Cannot use variable in custom expression, it hasn't been encountered yet` (NiagaraHlslTranslator FinalResolveNamespacedTokens). Only dataset-backed `Particles.*` writes work from custom nodes; pre-declaring the variable in other scripts does not satisfy the check. This blocks any custom-node count-bridge between emitters.
- **Cross-emitter enable gating**: the Fragments emitter stayed disabled (0 spawned) through every fix round — `EmitterState Loop Duration` default 1s completes new emitters, the RI param exists in *two* copies (SystemUpdateScript and EmitterUpdateScript stores; batch edits can update only one), and cross-emitter `Engine.<Emitter>.NumParticles` links never bound at runtime. Removing the emitter with a live component still referencing it crashed the editor (`Array index out of bounds`) — destroy live actors *before* emitter removal.

M3-v1 therefore uses the droplets themselves: `SP_CharInteract`'s second HLSL node (`SplitKill`) latches `Particles.Lifetime = Age + 1.4` when a fast character (`CSpeed > ScatterVelThreshold`) passes within 15 cm of the capsule surface. The existing scatter impulse throws the hit droplet outward and it pops 1.4 s later — a burst impression with zero new emitters. A true 5-way fragment split moves to M3-v2 via Niagara Data Channels (backlog).

### Tool bug #1: star-splice in add_niagara_scratch_pad_parameter_write (fixed)

The tool spliced every new MapSet in parallel off the Input node (star topology): only the last-added MapSet reached Output; earlier writes (Velocity!) were silently orphaned. Unconditional-kill probes returning zero deaths exposed it. Fixed in `UnrealMCPNiagaraCommands_Part4.cpp` to chain-splice (previous chain tail → new MapSet → Output). SP_CharInteract rebuilt (4th time) with the fixed tool; verified chain: Input → MapSet(Velocity) → MapSet(Lifetime) → Output.

### Tool bug #2: replace_niagara_custom_hlsl left ChangeId stale (fixed)

After the rebuild, the radius-200 gate still returned zero deaths, and *unconditional* kill HLSL also did nothing. Editor log showed the smoking gun: the first compile after a replace took 0.3–1.5 s (real translation), but every subsequent replace + RequestCompile finished in ~0.01 s — a no-op, with the GPU still running stale bytecode. Root cause: `SetNiagaraCustomHlslString` (UnrealMCPNiagaraCommandsShared.h) wrote the `CustomHlsl` property via reflection without `MarkNodeRequiresSynchronization`, so the node ChangeId never updated and RequestCompile saw the scripts as synchronized. Fix: mark synchronization (with graph-recompile raise) after the property write. Verified post-fix: two consecutive replaces in one session both retranslated for real (0.81 s, 0.68 s).

**Gate methodology lesson**: after any `replace_niagara_custom_hlsl` + compile, confirm a real retranslation in the log (`LogNiagara: Compiling System ... took N sec`; N < 0.05 s means no-op) before trusting runtime numbers. Every pre-fix "zero deaths" measurement this session was invalid for this reason.

### Housekeeping findings

- The Droplets particle-update chain carries orphaned module-input override MapSets from removed probe/rebuild modules (`SP_KillProbe_0`, `SP_FloatProbe_0`, `SP_VelProbe_0`, `SP_CharInteract_1`, plus stale `SP_CharInteract_0` copies). They only write module-namespace inputs, so they are functionally inert, but they bloat the graph — cleanup is backlog (likely same root as the star-splice era; `remove_niagara_modules` does not sweep override MapSets it did not create).
- `remove_niagara_modules` gained `include_system_modules` (Part2.cpp + Python wrapper): with no emitter filter it also walks the shared system spawn/update graph (reports emitter_name "System", emitter_index -1). Verified live by removing 3 system-stack probe modules.
- Viewport-realtime override runs on a 300-frame budget; editor gate windows longer than ~5 s must re-request realtime mid-window or the sim silently freezes (age stops advancing) and later delivers a large catch-up delta (see §18 artifact).

### Gate result (PASS)

Radius temporarily widened 15 → 200 cm for measurability. Fresh `NA_M3Gate4` actor at origin, continuous realtime, fake character injected at (0,0,0) with velocity (600,0,0) (> threshold 250), capsule (42,90). Result: total_spawned 314 vs num_particles 298 — **16 delayed deaths** (expected ~6 % of population inside the 2.42 m kill capsule; attraction feeds more in). Radius restored to 15, real recompile confirmed (0.68 s), asset saved. Map dirty flags cleared; no test actors persisted.

Awaiting user visual review in PIE: run through the droplet swarm fast — directly hit droplets should fly off and pop after ~1.4 s.

## 21. M3-v2 death-site burst via Niagara Data Channel (2026-07-11)

User visual feedback on M3-v1: droplets disappear, but nothing pops at the vanish point. M3-v2 adds real burst particles at each death location using NDC — the first working GPU->CPU Niagara Data Channel pipeline in this repo.

### Architecture

1. **`NDC_WaterBlobBurst`** (NiagaraDataChannelAsset, Global handler, KeepPreviousFrameData): variables `Position` (Position) + `Velocity` (Vector).
2. **Droplets GPU write**: `SP_CharInteract` gained a chained `Particles.NDCDoBurst` (bool) MapSet fed by a custom-HLSL death-frame detector (`Lifetime < 90000 && (Lifetime - Age) <= DeltaTime`), followed by a spliced **DI function-call node** `Append(NDCWrite, Emit=NDCDoBurst, Position, Velocity)`. The write DI (stack-local override) publishes to Game+CPU with `AllocationCount 64`.
3. **`NS_WaterBurst`** (CPU, one-shot): burst 8 sprites, radial From-Point velocity 250, lifetime 0.5 s, sprite 14, SystemState loop Once -> completes -> auto-destroys. Verified in-editor: spawn -> burst -> auto-destroyed in under 3 s.
4. **BP consume**: `BP_WaterBlobManager` Tick tail appends `ReadFromNiagaraDataChannel(NDC, prev frame)` -> `Num`-1 -> ForLoop -> `ReadPosition("Position", i)` -> `SpawnSystemAtLocation(NS_WaterBurst, pos)`. BP compile clean.

### New MCP tooling (this session)

- **`configure_niagara_data_channel_asset`**: creates/updates an NDC asset (Global channel object, variable layout via reflection `CopyCompleteValue`, KeepPreviousFrameData). Python couldn't set `FNiagaraTypeDefinition`, hence C++.
- **`add_niagara_scratch_pad_di_function_call`**: splices a signature-only `UNiagaraNodeFunctionCall` for a DI member function into a scratch-pad chain, extending the copied signature with **variadic inputs** (pins beyond `RequiredInputs`), wiring each from a `ParameterMapGet` (`Module.NDCWrite`, `Particles.*`). Exec map pins spliced tail -> node -> Output.
- Type parser (`TryParseNiagaraTypeDefinition`) gained a generic `/Script/...` class-path fallback for any `UNiagaraDataInterface` subclass; `insert_niagara_scratch_pad_custom_hlsl_for_pin`'s `parameter_inputs` now accepts DI types.

### Hard-won findings

- **Custom HLSL cannot call variadic DI functions** (`Append`): the translator emits a stub with an EMPTY variadic parameter list (`(bool bEmit, , out bool bOutSuccess)` — compile error) because variadic parameter names/types come from graph function-call node pins, which custom HLSL call sites don't have. A real function-call node with signature-extended inputs compiles clean on GPU.
- **Write DI `AllocationCount` defaults to 0** — every GPU Append is silently dropped. Symptom: deaths confirmed, channel reads 0 forever, no warnings. Set an explicit per-frame allocation (64).
- **DI property edits do NOT dirty the compile** (0.008 s no-op `RequestCompile`); the runtime uses the DI snapshot from the last REAL compile. After changing DI properties, force a genuine retranslation (e.g. touch the HLSL text — the §20 sync fix makes that reliable) before trusting runtime behavior.
- **GPU->CPU NDC readback works in the editor world** in 5.6 (bPublishToCPU/Game + readback in `FNiagaraDataChannelDataProxy::BeginFrame`); gate observed 18 entries across 6 frames with plausible death positions (scattered droplets pop away from the kill zone, as designed).
- Editor-world game-side NDC roundtrip (BP write -> BP read) also verified — useful as an isolation probe.
- `remove_niagara_parameter_map_set_nodes` walks only emitter graphs, not scratch pads (matched 0 on a scratch MapSet); `insert_..._custom_hlsl_for_pin` replace path requires the preserve-link input name to match an existing single-linked input (`preserve_existing_link_as="Map"` works for context nodes).

### Gates

Niagara compile clean (real 0.77 s retranslation, zero errors); NDC write gate PASS (18 entries observed game-side); NS_WaterBurst one-shot completes and auto-destroys; BP compile clean; py gates PASS. Awaiting PIE visual review: run through the swarm — hit droplets fly off and POP with an 8-sprite radial burst ~1.4 s later.

Known watch item: if bursts visually duplicate per death (NDC entry visible over multiple frames in some worlds), add a consumed-marker or switch the BP read to current-frame only.

## 22. Spawn floor = spline plane + spline persistence trap (2026-07-11)

User report: particles spawned below the spline. Two causes, both fixed.

### Spawn/containment floor

- `SP_PolygonLocation`: spawn z = BaseZ + `lerp(max(SpawnHeightMin,0), max(SpawnHeightMax,MinH), rand)`. `SpawnHeightMin` now means "clearance above the spline plane"; **BP default changed 100 -> 0** (CDO and the live instance), so the lower bound is exactly the spline height. Upper bound remains the `SpawnHeightMax` parameter.
- `SP_DriftBob` hard floor: `FloorZ = BaseZ + max(SpawnHeightMin,0)`; below the floor the up-spring is 4x stronger (8.0) and the final output velocity clamps its downward component to 0 at/below the floor. The <3-point fallback branch gets the same floor at the actor plane (soft ceiling at +300 unchanged), so scatter impulses cannot push droplets through the floor in either mode.
- `BoxLocation` Box Offset (0,0,0) -> (0,0,300): the editor-preview fallback spawn box now sits above the actor plane (z 0..600). This was the actual "below the spline" source the user saw — their hand-placed manager still had the default 2-point spline, so the symmetric fallback box spawned half its particles below the plane.
- Verified: real retranslations (1.28 s / 0.66 s) clean; V8 + BP saved.

### Spline persistence trap (recurred twice — treat as a standing rule)

- **Symptom**: the SpawnZone spline silently resets to the CDO default 2-point 1 m stub ("the spline disappeared").
- **Cause**: SplineComponent per-instance curve edits survive construction-script reruns ONLY when `bSplineHasBeenEdited=true`. Viewport hand-editing sets the flag automatically; **script APIs (`set_spline_points` / `add_spline_point`) do NOT**. Un-flagged edits are wiped by (a) map load and (b) **any Blueprint recompile that reinstances the actor** — e.g. changing a BP variable default.
- **Rule**: whenever seeding a spline from script, also `sp.set_editor_property('bSplineHasBeenEdited', True)`. After edits, the held Python component reference can go stale mid-call (reads 0 points) because reinstancing swaps the component — re-fetch the component from the actor before verifying.
- State: WaterBlobTest map instance carries a flagged 4-point quad (save + reload verified). The user's session-local manager in Template/Empty was reseeded and flagged (that map is never saved by policy).

## 23. Spline-range spawn restoration (2026-07-12)

User finding after the V8 revert: the placed spline survived, but the droplet population still looked box-shaped. The spline Niagara modules from §18-19 were intact; the failure was in the BP handoff and activation order.

- **Curve sampling fix**: `BP_WaterBlobManager` no longer sends only the four spline control points. Both Construction Script and BeginPlay now sample 32 evenly spaced positions with `GetLocationAtDistanceAlongSpline(World)` over `[0, SplineLength)`, so the closing point is not duplicated and curved tangents are represented in `User.SpawnPolygon`.
- **Initial burst ordering fix**: `BlobFX` auto-activates before BeginPlay. The previous BeginPlay-only upload therefore let the long-lived initial burst use the `<3 points` BoxLocation fallback. BeginPlay now calls `ReinitializeSystem` immediately after uploading the 32-point array.
- **Editor preview/persistence fix**: Construction Script uploads the same 32 samples and `SpawnZone.bInputSplinePointsToConstructionScript=true`. Existing per-instance spline edits remain protected by `bSplineHasBeenEdited=true` (§22).
- **Scope lock honored**: V8's `SP_PolygonLocation`/`SP_DriftBob` HLSL, water surface, Ground/Snow, and character-interaction logic were not changed. `NS_WaterBlob_V8` and `M_WaterDroplet_MaskedDither` remain the active system/material.

