## 31. M3.5 GPU distance-field world collision and wall response (2026-07-13)

The approved WaterBlob system now collides with world geometry through Niagara GPU Distance Fields and visibly rebounds from vertical walls while keeping the existing floor-rest behavior. Finished-content changes were limited to the main-droplet collision configuration and the Low-quality Global Distance Field prerequisites needed by that path; the editor-only UnrealMCP static-switch authoring route was also hardened. The spline spawn, character attraction/scatter, fixed 96-particle controller, exact death replacement, masked spawn fade, per-particle height spring, Burst renderer/material, Burst source-velocity inheritance, and the ReactiveSurface Water/Ground/Snow content were preserved.

### Final production collision contract

`NS_WaterBlob_V8` uses GPU Distance Field Collision with these final values:

- Particle Radius `15`.
- Restitution `0.8`, Dynamic Friction `0.25`, and Bounce Collision Friction `0`.
- Rest State enabled and controlled by surface normal.
- Rest Normal Alignment Percentage `0.75` with Valid Rest Normal `(0,0,1)`.
- Kill Particles Lodged Within Meshes enabled.
- Correct Interpenetration disabled.
- Max Collision Count disabled, so its stored count does not participate in runtime behavior.
- The existing collision-normal randomization override remains `0.1`.
- Fixed Tick Delta Time remains disabled.

The root cause of the missing wall bounce was not restitution alone. A collision could calculate a reflected velocity and then satisfy the Rest path in the same tick, which replaced that velocity with zero. Surface-normal-controlled Rest excludes vertical walls from the floor-rest condition while retaining rest on upward-facing surfaces; restitution `0.8` then makes the remaining rebound visible. Particle Radius `15` was the smallest tested value that removed wall/slab tunneling in the low-frame-rate stress fixture.

### Low scalability prerequisite

`MCPGameProject/Config/DefaultScalability.ini` now adds only these values to `[GlobalIlluminationQuality@0]`:

```ini
r.AOQuality=1
r.DistanceFieldAO=1
```

The project already has `r.GenerateMeshDistanceFields=True`. Low continues to use `sg.GlobalIlluminationQuality=0`, `r.DynamicGlobalIlluminationMethod=0`, and `r.Lumen.DiffuseIndirect.Allow=0`; this change does not enable Lumen or raise the whole Low GI preset. It does enable renderer-wide Global Distance Field/Distance Field AO prerequisites, so its GPU and memory cost is global rather than isolated to WaterBlob. Target-hardware profiling remains a shipping gate.

### Reusable static-switch authoring hardening

The existing editor-only `set_niagara_module_static_switch` command was strengthened because Niagara collision mode is a compile-time enum switch rather than a RapidIteration value. Discovery now reports the pin's actual enum path, display/authored/internal names, exact numeric values, current-value validity, and known hidden state while preserving the legacy internal-name `options` array. Writes canonicalize display/internal/full/authored names or exact integers against the real `UEnum`, reject Hidden/Spacer/MAX entries, support an exact stale-current-value contract, and block the safe Python save path unless the compile actually launches, reaches idle, retains the post-request Compile IDs and canonical pin readback, leaves every affected script ready, and synchronizes each final cached compiled-data ID with its final computed ID. The command stores only native Niagara pin data; it does not serialize an UnrealMCP runtime dependency into the authored system.

### Rejected alternatives and regression finding

- Correct Interpenetration worsened the 3 Hz canary from 33 penetrations among 56 candidates to 38 among 42 and was rejected.
- GPU trace-vector/max-trace-length overrides did not affect the active Global Distance Field branch.
- Increasing Max Collision Count could not help while the feature remained disabled.
- Restitution or friction changes alone could not prevent the same-tick Rest overwrite.
- Particle Radius `10` was insufficient at low frame rate; a global GDF-resolution increase had disproportionate project-wide cost.
- Custom HLSL, CPU Collision, Hardware Ray Tracing, and disabling Rest completely expanded behavior/platform risk without being required.
- A Fixed Tick/substep canary improved collision sampling but caused the fixed-population system to grow from 96 to 142 because `SpawnPerFrame` replayed the same replenish pulse on multiple substeps. Fixed Tick was therefore rejected rather than promoted.

### Final verification gates

| Gate | Evidence | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 3 Hz production stress | 60 frames over about 19.67 seconds at `DeltaTime=0.3333334`; 62 wall-side candidates; penetration 0; slab pass-through 0; rebound 34; disappearance 0. The transient wall-stress cohort remained `92 -> 92`; the restored full-system 96-count contract is recorded in the separate regression row below. | PASS |
| 3 Hz canary parity | Penetration 0; slab pass-through 0; rebound 36; disappearance 0 | PASS |
| 60 FPS-capped / 240-frame canary | 31 contact particles; penetration 0; slab pass-through 0; recorded median delta `0.025884` seconds | PASS |
| Full WaterBlob regression | 21 samples over 10.003 seconds, all exactly 96 live; 32 sampled spline points; all initial particles inside the sampled polygon | PASS |
| Actual character/NDC path | SpectatorPawn movement produced 38 deaths and 38 replacement particle IDs; live count recovered from 87 to 96; at most 38 Burst components and 0 after the quiet window | PASS |
| Niagara compile | Production system error 0, warning 0, not dirty, no outstanding compile | PASS |
| Runtime log | Dedicated final slice contained 0 Blueprint Runtime Error, Accessed None, Niagara error/warning, ensure, or fatal hits | PASS |
| Temp cleanup | QA walls/canary assets absent; SIE stopped; manager transform and editor settings restored | PASS |
| UnrealMCP authoring/tool gates | UE 5.6 editor target build; Python compile/import/Ruff; 354 tools including 82 Niagara tools; response-schema tests; 14/14 Niagara offline gates; generic live bridge smoke. A disposable V8 copy passed display-name mutation with three affected scripts ready and cached/computed IDs synchronized; a compile-disabled mutation returned `saved=false` with an unchanged disk SHA; numeric-enum no-op and stale-value rejection passed; canary deleted. Production V8 display-name no-op made no mutation/save. | PASS |

The final single-workstation GPU snapshot reported a 3.49 ms frame, 0.513 ms total `NiagaraGpuComputeDispatch`, 0.012 ms `UpdateDistanceFieldAtlas`, and 0.163 ms `DistanceFieldLighting`. These are editor-wide samples that include other Niagara and renderer work, not isolated WaterBlob costs or a before/after claim.

