## 32. WaterBlob V1 baseline closure (2026-07-13)

User version decision: all approved behavior and verification through §31 is the **WaterBlob V1** baseline.

V1 includes:

- Closed-spline sampled spawning with 32 runtime polygon points and polygon-aware containment.
- Character attraction/scatter with capsule-safe Empty-map fallback and five BP tuning scalars.
- Fixed 96-particle population, exact death-edge replacement, and no passive population growth.
- Masked-dither spawn fade and per-particle soft vertical spring around each spawn height.
- Death-site NDC Burst with the approved Mesh renderer/material, 50% tuned fragment scale, and source-velocity inheritance.
- Cleaned Niagara graph/renderer state with no stale WaterBlob canary assets.
- GPU Distance Field world collision, floor Rest behavior, and visually approved vertical-wall rebound.
- Low-quality GDF prerequisites, reusable static-switch authoring hardening, full build/tool/live gates, and the final clean runtime log gate.

V1 does not include M4 particle merging, a PAR/NeighborGrid3D neighbor solution, or M5 promotion into a tracked production content root. Low-preset profiling on representative target hardware also remains an M5 shipping gate because the GDF/DFAO prerequisite has renderer-wide cost.

Version boundary and preservation rules:

- The §31 hash manifest is the exact local V1 asset manifest; a final recheck found every listed WaterBlob, Empty, Water, Ground, and Snow hash unchanged.
- `/Game/Template/Empty` remains the unsaved starting test map and has zero dirty packages in the final editor preflight.
- WaterBlob binaries remain under Git-ignored `/Game/_MCP_Temp`. The tracked V1 checkpoint therefore contains the verified report, Low configuration, and reusable MCP tooling, but not the generated `.uasset` binaries.
- M4 experiments must start from a copy/canary and must not silently overwrite the V1 hash baseline. M5 promotion must explicitly copy the accepted V1 result into a tracked production root and rerun dependency, restart, cook, and target-hardware gates.

WaterBlob V1 verdict: **CLOSED / PASS**. Future feature work starts after this boundary.

## 33. Multi-manager isolation and tracked-content promotion (2026-07-13)

The V1 runtime is no longer limited to one manager per level. Each `BP_WaterBlobManager` creates two non-zero random integer IDs at BeginPlay, writes both IDs into its Niagara component, and accepts a death Data Channel row only when both row IDs match its own pair. The two-key match makes accidental cross-manager acceptance negligibly unlikely while keeping the existing global Niagara Data Channel architecture.

The promoted event contract is:

- `NDC_WaterBlobBurst`: `Position`, `Velocity`, `ManagerID_A`, `ManagerID_B`.
- `NS_WaterBlob_V8`: writes the manager IDs with every death row and otherwise preserves the accepted spline spawn, attraction/scatter, population, fade, spring, and collision graphs.
- `BP_WaterBlobManager`: validates the reader and both integer reads, counts only matching rows, spawns Burst only for matching rows, and writes the exact matched count to `User.ReplenishCount`.
- Every manager independently maintains its own `TargetCount` contract, default 96. Death rows from another manager neither spawn Burst nor replenish the local system.

### Production content

The tested closure was promoted from the generated canary into tracked native Unreal content:

- `/Game/WaterBlob/Blueprints/BP_WaterBlobManager`
- `/Game/WaterBlob/Niagara/Systems/NS_WaterBlob_V8`
- `/Game/WaterBlob/Niagara/Systems/NS_WaterBurst`
- `/Game/WaterBlob/Niagara/DataChannels/NDC_WaterBlobBurst`
- `/Game/WaterBlob/Materials/M_WaterDroplet_MaskedDither`

All internal WaterBlob references were remapped to these production paths. Asset Registry closure, binary marker scanning, cook, and an isolated load with `UnrealMCP` disabled found no `_MCP_Temp` or UnrealMCP dependency in the production closure.

